Figure 4.1: Per-program tile coverage of a length-N 1D tensor by BLOCK_SIZE lanes. Steel-blue cells are in-bounds lanes (the kernel reads/writes them); light-gray cells are masked-off tail lanes that the kernel’s mask must guard against. Numbers inside cells are global element indices; ‘x’ marks masked lanes.





4.4.1 Pointer arithmetic and masks
x_ptr is a pointer to fp32 (when x.dtype == torch.float32). Adding an integer offset advances the pointer in element units, not bytes. So x_ptr + offsets is a (BLOCK_SIZE,) tensor of pointers, each pointing at the corresponding element of x. tl.load takes a pointer (or a tile of pointers) and returns the value(s) at those addresses. The mask argument suppresses loads on lanes where mask is false — those lanes return other= (defaulting to 0) and, crucially, do not perform the memory access. That last bit is the whole point of the mask: it prevents out-of-bounds reads on the tail tile.
tl.store is symmetric. Masked lanes simply do not write.
other= matters for reductions, not for elementwise. In Chapter 4 you will see why other=0 is right for sums and other=-inf is right for maxes. For vector_add the default other=0 is fine because we never read the masked lanes of the result.


4.4.2 Correctness test
The full test module is in tests/test_ch02_vector_add.py. The core of it:

@pytest.mark.parametrize("n", [7, 1024, 1023, 1025, 1_048_576])
def test_vector_add_fp32(n):
    x = torch.randn(n, device="cuda", dtype=torch.float32)
    y = torch.randn(n, device="cuda", dtype=torch.float32)
    out = vector_add(x, y)
    torch.testing.assert_close(out, x + y, rtol=1e-5, atol=1e-5)

n=7 is small enough to step through by hand. n=1023 and n=1025 force the tail-mask path on either side of the BLOCK_SIZE=1024 boundary. n=1_048_576 is a round number for the benchmark below.


4.4.3 Benchmark
triton.testing.do_bench runs the callable, warms up, syncs, and returns the median elapsed time in milliseconds. Use it.

import triton

def bench(x, y):
    return triton.testing.do_bench(lambda: vector_add(x, y))

# Effective bandwidth for fp32 vector_add:
#   2 loads + 1 store per element = 3 * N * 4 bytes
ms = bench(x, y)
gbps = 3 * x.numel() * 4 / (ms * 1e-3) / 1e9

For a 2070 SUPER (~448 GB/s peak), expect to see ~390–420 GB/s on a 4 MB-or-larger working set. Anything dramatically lower means the kernel is launch-bound (try larger N) or you forgot to warm up (do_bench does this for you, so don’t).






Placeholder data. The numbers below are hardcoded illustrative values, not measurements. Replace with output from your own triton.testing.do_bench run before drawing conclusions.




{
  const data = [
    {N: 4096,      triton_ms: 0.012, torch_ms: 0.015},
    {N: 65536,     triton_ms: 0.018, torch_ms: 0.022},
    {N: 1048576,   triton_ms: 0.085, torch_ms: 0.092},
    {N: 16777216,  triton_ms: 1.30,  torch_ms: 1.45},
  ];
  const long = data.flatMap(d =&gt; [
    {N: d.N, impl: "triton", ms: d.triton_ms},
    {N: d.N, impl: "torch",  ms: d.torch_ms}
  ]);
  return Plot.plot({
    color: {legend: true, domain: ["triton", "torch"], range: ["steelblue", "orange"]},
    x: {type: "log", label: "N (elements)"},
    y: {label: "median latency (ms) — lower is better"},
    marks: [
      Plot.line(long, {x: "N", y: "ms", stroke: "impl"}),
      Plot.dot(long,  {x: "N", y: "ms", fill: "impl"})
    ]
  });
}








Figure 4.2: Vector-add median latency vs tensor length N (log x-axis). Lower is better. Compares the Triton kernel (steelblue) against eager torch.add (orange).






4.4.4 Optimization discussion
The only knob in vector_add is BLOCK_SIZE. The benchmark script sweeps [64, 128, 256, 1024, 4096]. The typical pattern on a modern NVIDIA GPU:

BLOCK_SIZE=64: many small programs, launch overhead dominates at small N, bandwidth flat at large N.
BLOCK_SIZE=256: usually a good balance.
BLOCK_SIZE=1024: the default in this book for elementwise. It is the right answer often enough that you should start here and only deviate with a measurement to justify it.
BLOCK_SIZE=4096: more registers per program, fewer programs total, occupancy may drop, marginal change for elementwise.

The right answer depends on the GPU and on N. For real production code use triton.autotune (Chapter 26). For learning, a fixed default plus a manual sweep teaches you more.
The kernel is memory-bound. The compute (one add per element) is trivial; you are limited by how fast you can stream from HBM. The bound is the peak HBM bandwidth of your GPU times whatever fraction of it the access pattern can achieve. Coalesced, contiguous reads at BLOCK_SIZE &gt;= 256 typically hit 90 %+ of peak.


4.4.5 Common bugs

Forgot the mask. Without mask=mask on tl.load, the tail program reads past the end of the tensor. You may get garbage values or a memory fault depending on alignment. Always add the mask, even when you “know” N is a multiple of BLOCK_SIZE — production code does not know.
BLOCK_SIZE not tl.constexpr. Without the annotation, Triton cannot specialize the loop bounds at compile time and you get either a confusing compile error or, worse, a slow kernel. The annotation lives on the parameter, not on a Python variable inside the kernel.
Passing a non-contiguous tensor without strides. The kernel above assumes contiguous storage. If you pass x.t() or x[::2], the data is no longer at consecutive addresses and x_ptr + offsets walks into the wrong cells. Chapter 3 handles strides explicitly.
Wrong grid math. (n_elements // BLOCK_SIZE,) drops the tail tile. Use triton.cdiv(n, BLOCK_SIZE).



4.4.6 Exercises
See exercises/ch02_exercises.md for the full text; here are the prompts in brief:
Beginner: (B1) vector_sub. (B2) scalar_mul(x, alpha). (B3) Fused fma(x, y, z) = x * y + z.
Intermediate: (I1) clamp(x, lo, hi). (I2) where(cond, x, y) for a torch.bool mask tensor.
Advanced: (A1) vector_add_strided — accept non-contiguous 1-D inputs, pass strides as kernel args. A teaser for Chapter 3, where strides are first-class.
Worked solutions are in solutions/ch02_solutions.py. Try them blind first.

5.6 Kernel 2: row-wise bias add
out[i, j] = x[i, j] + bias[j] for x : (M, N), bias : (N,). One program per row.
Where this shows up. This is the + b half of every nn.Linear(in, out) layer: after the matmul x @ W.T produces an (M, N) activation, you broadcast a length-N bias vector across all M rows. The same pattern appears in attention QKV projections, the two MLP projections in each transformer block, and the final LM-head projection that turns hidden states into vocab logits. It also shows up outside nn.Linear — for example adding a positional-bias vector to attention scores, or adding a per-channel offset before an activation. The kernel matters as a teaching example because it forces you to load a 1-D tensor (bias) and a 2-D tensor (x) with different stride patterns in the same program: bias strides only along N, while x strides along both M (between rows, big jump) and N (within a row, contiguous). Understanding how those two pointer expressions coexist is the warm-up for every fused-bias kernel later in the book — Chapter 10 fuses exactly this op into matmul so the bias add costs zero extra memory traffic.

5.6.1 PyTorch reference

def row_add_ref(x, bias):
    return x + bias  # broadcast over rows



5.6.2 Triton implementation

@triton.jit
def row_add_kernel(
    x_ptr, bias_ptr, out_ptr,
    M, N,
    stride_xm, stride_xn,
    stride_om, stride_on,
    BLOCK_SIZE_N: tl.constexpr,
):
    pid_m = tl.program_id(axis=0)
    x_row = x_ptr + pid_m * stride_xm
    o_row = out_ptr + pid_m * stride_om

    for col_start in range(0, N, BLOCK_SIZE_N):
        cols = col_start + tl.arange(0, BLOCK_SIZE_N)
        mask = cols &lt; N
        x = tl.load(x_row + cols * stride_xn, mask=mask, other=0.0)
        b = tl.load(bias_ptr + cols, mask=mask, other=0.0)
        tl.store(o_row + cols * stride_on, x + b, mask=mask)

Two new ideas: (1) the kernel takes strides as runtime args, so it works on any 2-D layout the caller hands it; (2) there is an inner Python for loop over column tiles, because rows can be longer than BLOCK_SIZE_N. Triton unrolls that loop at compile time when N // BLOCK_SIZE_N is small; for large rows it stays as a runtime loop.


5.6.3 Shape trace
For M=32, N=4097, BLOCK_SIZE_N=1024:
Input:                       x    : (32, 4097)      fp32
Input:                       bias : (4097,)         fp32
Grid:                        (32,)
pid_m                        scalar                 int32   # 0..31
x_row = x_ptr + pid_m*N      ptr&lt;fp32&gt;              scalar
Inner loop iterations:       5  (cdiv(4097, 1024))
  iter 0: cols  = [0..1023]
          mask  = all True
          x     = (1024,)    fp32
          b     = (1024,)    fp32
          out   = (1024,)    fp32   stored to row pid_m, cols 0..1023
  iter 1..3: same shapes, no masking
  iter 4: cols  = [4096..5119]
          mask  = first 1 lane True, rest False
          x     = (1024,)    fp32   (only x[pid_m, 4096] is the real value)
          b     = (1024,)    fp32   (only bias[4096] is real)
          out   stored to row pid_m, col 4096 only


5.6.4 Pointer arithmetic and masks
x_row is a per-program scalar pointer at the start of this program’s row. The cols * stride_xn term is the within-row offset vector. For a contiguous input stride_xn == 1 and this is just cols; for a column-strided input it scales by whatever the column stride happens to be.
other=0 on both loads is fine because masked lanes are never stored. We could use any constant.


5.6.5 Interactive: row-wise bias add memory access
Pick a row (pid_m) and an inner-loop iteration (iter). The widget shows exactly which elements of x, bias, and out that program touches in that iteration, and which lanes the mask drops on the tail tile. Steelblue cells are loaded/stored this iteration; the lighter tinted row marks the entire row this program owns; gray cells are untouched. Crosshatched cells are masked off (out-of-bounds lanes).

viewof rowadd_M = Inputs.range([4, 16], {value: 6, step: 1, label: "M (rows of x)"})
viewof rowadd_N = Inputs.range([4, 40], {value: 17, step: 1, label: "N (cols of x, length of bias)"})
viewof rowadd_BLOCK_SIZE_N = Inputs.range([2, 16], {value: 8, step: 1, label: "BLOCK_SIZE_N"})
viewof rowadd_pid_m = Inputs.range([0, 15], {value: 2, step: 1, label: "pid_m (program / row)"})
viewof rowadd_iter = Inputs.range([0, 20], {value: 0, step: 1, label: "iter (inner-loop iteration)"})

{
  const M = rowadd_M;
  const N = rowadd_N;
  const BS = rowadd_BLOCK_SIZE_N;
  const pid_m = Math.min(rowadd_pid_m, M - 1);
  const n_iters = Math.ceil(N / BS);
  const iter = Math.min(rowadd_iter, n_iters - 1);

  const col_start = iter * BS;
  const stride_xm = N;
  const stride_xn = 1;
  const stride_om = N;
  const stride_on = 1;

  // Build x matrix data
  const xData = [];
  for (let i = 0; i &lt; M; i++) {
    for (let j = 0; j &lt; N; j++) {
      xData.push({
        i, j,
        addr: i * stride_xm + j * stride_xn,
        owned: i === pid_m,
        touched: false,
        masked: false
      });
    }
  }
  // Mark touched lanes for x and out
  const outData = xData.map(d =&gt; ({...d, addr: d.i * stride_om + d.j * stride_on, touched: false, masked: false}));
  for (let lane = 0; lane &lt; BS; lane++) {
    const j = col_start + lane;
    const inBounds = j &lt; N;
    // For x: only mark touched within the matrix; if j &gt;= N it's masked (we still highlight cells that exist)
    if (inBounds) {
      const idx = pid_m * N + j;
      xData[idx].touched = true;
      outData[idx].touched = true;
    }
  }
  // Count masked-off lanes this iteration
  let masked_lanes = 0;
  for (let lane = 0; lane &lt; BS; lane++) {
    const j = col_start + lane;
    if (j &gt;= N) masked_lanes++;
  }

  // Build bias data
  const biasData = [];
  for (let j = 0; j &lt; N; j++) {
    biasData.push({ j, addr: j, touched: false, masked: false });
  }
  for (let lane = 0; lane &lt; BS; lane++) {
    const j = col_start + lane;
    if (j &lt; N) biasData[j].touched = true;
  }

  // Build "phantom" masked cells for tail visualisation (drawn beyond the matrix edge)
  const xMasked = [];
  const biasMasked = [];
  const outMasked = [];
  for (let lane = 0; lane &lt; BS; lane++) {
    const j = col_start + lane;
    if (j &gt;= N) {
      xMasked.push({ i: pid_m, j, addr: pid_m * stride_xm + j * stride_xn });
      biasMasked.push({ j, addr: j });
      outMasked.push({ i: pid_m, j, addr: pid_m * stride_om + j * stride_on });
    }
  }

  // Maximum visible j for axes
  const jMax = Math.max(N, col_start + BS);

  const cellW = 28;
  const cellH = 22;
  const plotW = Math.max(360, jMax * cellW + 80);

  const colorFor = d =&gt; d.touched ? "steelblue" : (d.owned ? "#dbe7f3" : "#eeeeee");
  const colorForOut = d =&gt; d.touched ? "#3fa15a" : (d.owned ? "#e0eee4" : "#eeeeee");
  const textColor = d =&gt; d.touched ? "white" : "#444";

  const xPlot = Plot.plot({
    width: plotW,
    height: M * cellH + 60,
    marginLeft: 60,
    marginTop: 30,
    x: {label: "j (col)", domain: [0, jMax], axis: "top", ticks: d3.range(0, jMax + 1)},
    y: {label: "i (row)", domain: [M, 0], ticks: d3.range(0, M + 1)},
    marks: [
      Plot.rect(xData, {
        x1: d =&gt; d.j, x2: d =&gt; d.j + 1,
        y1: d =&gt; d.i, y2: d =&gt; d.i + 1,
        fill: colorFor,
        stroke: "white",
        strokeWidth: 1,
        title: d =&gt; `x[${d.i},${d.j}] addr = base + ${d.i}*${stride_xm} + ${d.j}*${stride_xn} = ${d.addr}`
      }),
      Plot.rect(xMasked, {
        x1: d =&gt; d.j, x2: d =&gt; d.j + 1,
        y1: d =&gt; d.i, y2: d =&gt; d.i + 1,
        fill: "lightgray",
        stroke: "gray",
        strokeDasharray: "2,2",
        strokeWidth: 1,
        title: d =&gt; `MASKED lane: would-be x[${d.i},${d.j}] addr=${d.addr} — not loaded (mask=False)`
      }),
      Plot.text(xData, {
        x: d =&gt; d.j + 0.5, y: d =&gt; d.i + 0.5,
        text: d =&gt; `${d.addr}`,
        fill: textColor, fontSize: 9
      }),
      Plot.text(xMasked, {
        x: d =&gt; d.j + 0.5, y: d =&gt; d.i + 0.5,
        text: "M", fill: "gray", fontSize: 9
      })
    ]
  });

  const biasPlot = Plot.plot({
    width: plotW,
    height: cellH + 60,
    marginLeft: 60,
    marginTop: 30,
    x: {label: "j (col of bias)", domain: [0, jMax], axis: "top", ticks: d3.range(0, jMax + 1)},
    y: {axis: null, domain: [1, 0]},
    marks: [
      Plot.rect(biasData, {
        x1: d =&gt; d.j, x2: d =&gt; d.j + 1,
        y1: 0, y2: 1,
        fill: d =&gt; d.touched ? "steelblue" : "#eeeeee",
        stroke: "white", strokeWidth: 1,
        title: d =&gt; `bias[${d.j}] addr = ${d.addr}`
      }),
      Plot.rect(biasMasked, {
        x1: d =&gt; d.j, x2: d =&gt; d.j + 1,
        y1: 0, y2: 1,
        fill: "lightgray",
        stroke: "gray",
        strokeDasharray: "2,2",
        strokeWidth: 1,
        title: d =&gt; `MASKED lane: would-be bias[${d.j}] — not loaded`
      }),
      Plot.text(biasData, {
        x: d =&gt; d.j + 0.5, y: 0.5,
        text: d =&gt; `${d.addr}`,
        fill: d =&gt; d.touched ? "white" : "#444",
        fontSize: 9
      }),
      Plot.text(biasMasked, {
        x: d =&gt; d.j + 0.5, y: 0.5,
        text: "M", fill: "gray", fontSize: 9
      })
    ]
  });

  const outPlot = Plot.plot({
    width: plotW,
    height: M * cellH + 60,
    marginLeft: 60,
    marginTop: 30,
    x: {label: "j (col)", domain: [0, jMax], axis: "top", ticks: d3.range(0, jMax + 1)},
    y: {label: "i (row)", domain: [M, 0], ticks: d3.range(0, M + 1)},
    marks: [
      Plot.rect(outData, {
        x1: d =&gt; d.j, x2: d =&gt; d.j + 1,
        y1: d =&gt; d.i, y2: d =&gt; d.i + 1,
        fill: colorForOut,
        stroke: "white",
        strokeWidth: 1,
        title: d =&gt; `out[${d.i},${d.j}] addr = base + ${d.i}*${stride_om} + ${d.j}*${stride_on} = ${d.addr}`
      }),
      Plot.rect(outMasked, {
        x1: d =&gt; d.j, x2: d =&gt; d.j + 1,
        y1: d =&gt; d.i, y2: d =&gt; d.i + 1,
        fill: "lightgray",
        stroke: "gray",
        strokeDasharray: "2,2",
        strokeWidth: 1,
        title: d =&gt; `MASKED lane: would-be out[${d.i},${d.j}] addr=${d.addr} — not stored`
      }),
      Plot.text(outData, {
        x: d =&gt; d.j + 0.5, y: d =&gt; d.i + 0.5,
        text: d =&gt; `${d.addr}`,
        fill: d =&gt; d.touched ? "white" : "#444",
        fontSize: 9
      }),
      Plot.text(outMasked, {
        x: d =&gt; d.j + 0.5, y: d =&gt; d.i + 0.5,
        text: "M", fill: "gray", fontSize: 9
      })
    ]
  });

  const summary = md`
**Iteration ${iter} of ${n_iters}** (pid_m = ${pid_m}, BLOCK_SIZE_N = ${BS}):
- \`col_start = iter * BLOCK_SIZE_N = ${col_start}\`
- \`cols = col_start + tl.arange(0, BLOCK_SIZE_N) = [${col_start}..${col_start + BS - 1}]\`
- \`mask = cols &lt; N\` drops **${masked_lanes} lane(s)** off the tail (N = ${N})
- Pointer math: \`x_row = x_ptr + pid_m * stride_xm = x_ptr + ${pid_m} * ${stride_xm} = x_ptr + ${pid_m * stride_xm}\`
- Load addresses: \`x_row + cols * stride_xn\` = base + [${Array.from({length: BS}, (_, k) =&gt; pid_m * stride_xm + (col_start + k) * stride_xn).join(", ")}]
- Bias addresses: \`bias_ptr + cols\` = bias + [${Array.from({length: BS}, (_, k) =&gt; col_start + k).join(", ")}]
`;

  return html`
    &lt;div style="display:flex; flex-direction:column; gap:0.6em;"&gt;
      &lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;x : (M, N)&lt;/strong&gt; &nbsp; — the input matrix; light tint = row this program owns; steelblue = lanes loaded this iteration; crosshatched = masked tail lanes&lt;/div&gt;
      ${xPlot}
      &lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;bias : (N,)&lt;/strong&gt; &nbsp; — every program in the grid reads the same bias vector&lt;/div&gt;
      ${biasPlot}
      &lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;out : (M, N)&lt;/strong&gt; &nbsp; — green = lanes stored this iteration&lt;/div&gt;
      ${outPlot}
      ${summary}
    &lt;/div&gt;
  `;
}












